  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...At the moment, there is a weak coastal trough\nthat is moving through the coastal Carolinas, bringing scatered\nto numerous showers producing wind gusts 20-30 knots. As the\ntrough and associated precip shifts north into ENC, it will\nencounter a more stable environment inland and showers should\ngradually weaken. However along the coast we still have plenty\nof instability and some moisture convergence, so showers should\nkeep their strength if not strengthen along the coast and for \noffshore waters through the early morning hours. Coverage of\nshowers along the ENC coast this morning is uncertain however. \n\nBeyond this morning, today the last day of a weak stalled \ntrough over the TN Valley before it further weakens and gets \nswept up by a stronger trough over the great lakes. This upper \nlevel forcing today, results in one last day of above average \nchances of showers and thunderstorms along and ahead of the sea \nbreeze. This is aided by the continued southerly flow and record\nhigh PWATs for the day observed in the KMHX soundings in the \npast 24 hours. While storm motions should be progressive, \nstorms will be capable of dropping a quick 1-2 inches, resulting\nin minor flooding concerns. If we get enough breaks in the\nclouds this afternoon, instability could climb to near 3,000\nJ/kg, which would bring a concern of wet microbursts. After we \nlose daytime heating, rain chances go down drastically. for\ntonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thursday onwards area returns to climo or below\nclimo PoPs as a lack of mid level shortwaves or significant \nforcing will be noted across Eastern NC. This will likely result\nin daily chances for showers and thunderstorms along the sea- \nbreeze, but once again these chances should be isolated at best \ngiven the overall setup. After a cool start to the week \ntemperatures begin to rebound back into the upper 80s to low 90s\ninland and mid 80s along the OBX.",
